  "notes": "The research names his signature contribution outright: the go-go pocket, \"16th-note percussion cross-rhythm\", brought into Bad Boy from DC. So the percussion block is the most-authored part of this model — six lanes at 6–12 hits a bar, three to six times the density any other entry on this axis carries — and it is the reason he belongs in the roster at all rather than being another Hitmen name. The second documented parameter is the swing: he is described as adding \"swing and melody… to otherwise rigid rap tracks\", which is 0.6 on the 16ths with a loose 0.66 quantize from playing keys, guitar and drums himself. The bass is live electric with melodic movement and explicitly no synth sub, so `independent_riff` at 0.42 with a five-interval passing-tone set. ⚠ **The progression families here are INFERRED and the research supports none of them by name.** It gives only \"4–8 bar progressions… with real voice-leading\" over a Curtis Mayfield / Barry White palette, so the four families are this author's reading of that palette; `voicing.topVoiceStepwise` and the 4/8-bar `phraseBars` are the parts that *are* researched. These are the first thing to delete if this model ever sounds more written than the records do. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it high-medium: the interview is detailed and first-person about method, and silent about numbers. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 50 to 47 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 48 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 3.",
